<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Babrak Karmal</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1929">1929</a>–<a href="/years/1996">1996</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Babrak Karmal was an Afghan communist politician and leader of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parcham">Parcham</a> faction of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-democratic-party-of-afghanistan">People's Democratic Party of Afghanistan</a>. He was installed in power by the Soviet invasion of December 1979, which killed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hafizullah-amin">Hafizullah Amin</a>, and led the Democratic Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a> during the first years of the war against the mujahideen. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> replaced him with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohammad-najibullah">Mohammad Najibullah</a> in 1986, and he died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/babrak-karmal.jpg" alt="Babrak Karmal" width="320" height="456" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: United Press International · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Babrak_Karmal_January_1980_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Babrak Karmal was born Sultan Hussein on 6 January 1929 in Kamari, a village in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a>. His father, Muhammad Hussein, was a general in the Royal Afghan Army, and the family was among the wealthier ones of Kabul. His ethnic background was disputed, as he variously claimed to be a Pashtun or a Tajik.</p><p>He was imprisoned from 1953 to 1956 for activities the government judged too radical, and there a fellow prisoner, Mir Akbar Khyber, introduced him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marxism">Marxism</a>. He took the name Babrak Karmal, which means Comrade of the Workers in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pashto">Pashto</a>, to distance himself from his bourgeois background. When the 1964 constitution legalised new parties, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-democratic-party-of-afghanistan">People's Democratic Party of Afghanistan</a> (PDPA) was founded in January 1965 in the home of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nur-muhammad-taraki">Nur Muhammad Taraki</a>. It soon split into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalq">Khalq</a>, led by Taraki and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hafizullah-amin">Hafizullah Amin</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parcham">Parcham</a>, led by Karmal.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Karmal was one of four <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-democratic-party-of-afghanistan">PDPA</a> members elected to the lower house in 1965 and sat until 1969. The Parchamites took part in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohammad-daoud-khan">Mohammad Daoud Khan</a>'s overthrow of the monarchy in 1973, but from the mid-1970s Daoud purged them. Karmal became Deputy Chairman of the Revolutionary Council after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saur-revolution">Saur Revolution</a> of 1978. The Khalqists then pushed the Parchamites aside. After a failed Parchamite coup Amin began a purge, and Karmal was sent into exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a>.</p><p>In December 1979 Soviet troops intervened in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>, assassinated Amin and installed Karmal as Chairman of the Revolutionary Council and Chairman of the Council of Ministers. He served as general secretary of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-democratic-party-of-afghanistan">PDPA</a> from 1979 to 1986. His government released 2,600 of 2,700 prisoners under a general amnesty, issued the Fundamental Principles of the Democratic Republic of Afghanistan and replaced the red Khalqist flag with one of black, red and green. Many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghans">Afghans</a> nonetheless saw him as a Soviet puppet.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Soviet leaders came to blame Karmal for the failure to consolidate the government. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mikhail-gorbachev">Mikhail Gorbachev</a> removed him in 1986 and put <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohammad-najibullah">Mohammad Najibullah</a> in his place, and Karmal went into exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>. Najibullah allowed him to return to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a> on 20 June 1991. After Najibullah's government fell on 16 April 1992, Karmal was based at Hairatan and was associated with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abdul-rashid-dostum">Abdul Rashid Dostum</a>.</p><p>He later called the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saur-revolution">Saur Revolution</a> a great crime, since the country had not been ready for revolution. He died of liver cancer in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>'s Central Clinical Hospital in early December 1996, on either 1 or 3 December according to different sources.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Babrak Karmal” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Hafizullah Amin killed Taraki in 1979, and in December the Soviet Union invaded, killing Amin and installing <b>Babrak Karmal</b> of the Parcham faction.</blockquote><a href="/state/democratic-republic-of-afghanistan">Democratic Republic of Afghanistan</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=34.53,69.17&amp;year=1972" aria-label="Kabul on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6307 913 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Babrak Karmal"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6757 1183h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6757 1183h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=34.53,69.17&amp;year=1972">What was here in 1972? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
